<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gotō Shinpei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1857">1857</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Goto Shinpei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gotō Shinpei (1857–1929) was a Japanese physician and statesman. As head of civil administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> from 1898 to 1906 under Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodama-gentaro">Kodama Gentarō</a>, he organised land surveys, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a>, public health, railways and state monopolies. He then became the first president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-manchuria-railway">South Manchuria Railway</a>, served as home and foreign minister, and after the 1923 earthquake directed the plan for rebuilding Tokyo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/goto-shinpei.jpg" alt="Gotō Shinpei" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shimpei_Got%C5%8D.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gotō was born on 24 July 1857 at Isawa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-province">Mutsu Province</a>, the son of Gotō Sanetaka, a retainer of the Rusu clan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Date of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sendai">Sendai</a>. After Sendai's defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> the family gave up its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> status and farmed. He studied at the Sukagawa medical school in Fukushima, served as a government medic in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-rebellion">Satsuma Rebellion</a> of 1877, and at 25 became president of the Nagoya Medical School. Joining the Home Ministry's medical bureau in 1883, he studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and published his Principles of National Health in 1890.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodama-gentaro">Kodama Gentarō</a> made him chief of the Army Quarantine Office for the return of more than 230,000 soldiers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> and later took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. There Gotō replaced the military police with a civilian force, built a hospital and medical college in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taipei</a>, and licensed opium sales, rewarding loyal Taiwanese elites and undermining the armed resistance of the Yiminjun; registered addicts fell from 165,000 in 1900 to fewer than 8,000 by 1941. From the Qing baojia he built the Hoko system, which fined a group of ten households for a member's serious crime. Police stations in the aboriginal regions ran schools to assimilate children into Japanese culture. By 1905 the colony needed no support from the home government.</p><p>Gotō was Minister of Communications from 1908, helped found the Rikken Dōshikai, and as foreign minister in 1918 strongly backed the intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. In April 1919 he defended the violent suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement">March First Movement</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He became mayor of Tokyo City in 1920 and was again home minister in 1923, when he contributed to rebuilding the city after the Great Kantō earthquake. He was also the first Chief Scout of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the first director-general of NHK, and he named the first watch of Citizen's forerunner in 1924. Created a count in 1928, he died of a cerebral haemorrhage at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a> on 13 April 1929; his papers are kept at the Gotō Shinpei Memorial Museum in Mizusawa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gotō Shinpei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Governor-General Kodama Gentarō and his civil administrator <b>Gotō Shinpei</b> from 1898, the colony carried out land surveys and censuses, built railways and ports, and developed sugar, rice and camphor for the Empire of Japan, while policing heavily.</blockquote><a href="/state/taiwan-under-japanese-rule">Taiwan under Japanese rule</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mizusawa, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in University Hospital, Kyoto Prefectural University of Medicine, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.15,141.14&amp;year=1903" aria-label="Mizusawa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gotō Shinpei"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4415 4199h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4415 4199h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.15,141.14&amp;year=1903">What was here in 1903? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
